1. PURPOSE

Series 400 Command Engine defines the execution orchestration interface layer of the CommandCenter graph system.

It establishes:

how user or system commands enter the intelligence graph
how commands are interpreted as structured intent
how commands are validated against governance rules
how execution pathways are selected and routed across system modules

It does NOT define low-level execution logic or runtime systems.

2. CORE COMMAND ENGINE PRINCIPLE

All system actions MUST originate as a structured command interpreted through the graph intelligence system.

Command Engine ensures:

controlled execution
traceable intent interpretation
governance-compliant routing
deterministic execution pathways

No command may bypass graph interpretation.

3. COMMAND INPUT MODEL

Commands enter the system as structured intent objects:

3.1 USER COMMANDS

Definition:
Direct instructions originating from external operators.

Examples:

“ingest client data”
“generate dossier”
“analyze relationship cluster”

Characteristics:

externally initiated
must be validated
highest governance scrutiny
3.2 SYSTEM COMMANDS

Definition:
Internally generated instructions from system layers.

Examples:

memory update triggers
learning correction requests
prediction recalibration signals

Characteristics:

internally sourced
governance-bound
feedback-driven
3.3 ENGINE COMMANDS

Definition:
Instructions generated by analytical or reasoning layers.

Examples:

recommendation execution request
graph restructuring suggestion
temporal correction trigger

Characteristics:

derived commands
intelligence-generated
require validation
4. COMMAND INTERPRETATION PIPELINE

All commands MUST follow structured interpretation:

Raw Command Input
    ↓
Intent Parsing Layer
    ↓
Graph Context Binding Layer
    ↓
Governance Validation Layer
    ↓
Execution Path Selection Layer
    ↓
Module Routing Layer
    ↓
Execution Trigger Output

Each stage MUST be completed sequentially.

5. INTENT PARSING RULE

The system MUST convert raw commands into structured intent:

action type
target nodes
affected edges or clusters
temporal constraints
governance sensitivity level

Unparsed commands are invalid.

6. GRAPH CONTEXT BINDING RULE

All commands MUST be bound to:

relevant nodes (0400)
relationships (0401)
clusters (0402)
memory states (0403)
temporal context (0404)

Unbound commands are considered incomplete intelligence inputs.

7. GOVERNANCE VALIDATION RULE

All commands MUST pass governance constraints:

structural integrity compliance (Series 000)
memory immutability rules (0403)
graph consistency rules (0400–0402)
temporal alignment rules (0404)

Invalid commands MUST be rejected before execution.

8. EXECUTION PATH SELECTION RULE

The system MUST determine execution route based on:

command type
graph dependency structure
system load context (Kernel constraints)
temporal priority (0404)
governance sensitivity

Execution paths are deterministic and traceable.

9. MODULE ROUTING RULE

Validated commands are routed to:

ingestion module (if input intake required)
cases module (if structured workflows required)
engine systems (if analytical processing required)
vault module (if persistence required)

Routing MUST be explicit and logged in memory (0403).

10. COMMAND SAFETY BOUNDARY

Command Engine MUST enforce:

no direct graph mutation without validation
no memory overwrite without versioning
no edge creation without relationship validation
no learning updates without governance approval

Execution is always constrained, never autonomous.

11. FEEDBACK LOOP INTEGRATION

Command Engine integrates feedback from:

reasoning outputs (0406)
predictions (0405)
learning corrections (0407)

This ensures continuous refinement of execution pathways.

